      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 506,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Formula:** $H_2O$\n* **Structure:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**. \n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (water in oceans, rivers, and rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice in glaciers, snow, and hail)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, and humidity in the air)\n\n### 2. The Physical Properties\nWater has several unique and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it carries vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through ecosystems and living bodies.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Ice Expansion:** Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats, allowing marine life to survive under frozen lakes during the winter.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **The Source of Life:** All known life depends on water. It makes up about 60% of the human body and roughly 71% of the Earth's surface (mostly in oceans). \n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly recycled through the Earth. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to the ground as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into bodies of water. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\nIn short, water is the driving force of nature, weather, and life itself."
